# A receiver that nobody owns is still freed exactly once.
#
# `make()??.clone()` produces a `Bag` no binding names. The clone is a deep copy, so
# after it the original's buffer belongs to nobody: 16 bytes leaked, in 1 block, with the
# program still exiting 0. Only the interposer can see it.
#
# The shape did not compile at all until a `??` receiver could resolve its type, so this
# is the cost of that and it is paid at the same place. The rule is the one the language
# already uses for a discarded owning temporary: register it as a scope temp and let
# scope-exit RAII free it. Rust and C++ drop such a temporary at the end of the enclosing
# STATEMENT, which is tighter; scope exit is later, never earlier, so it is safe.
#
# The struct owns a dynamic array because that is the cheapest field whose leak the
# interposer can count. `.clone()` is what makes the leak observable: it takes a deep copy
# and leaves the original with no owner at all.
# EXPECT_RUNTIME_EXIT: 0
# EXPECT_STDOUT_EXACT: "n=3 first=1\n"
# EXPECT_NO_LEAKS: true

struct Bag:
    i32[] items

fn make() Bag:
    return Result.Ok(Bag(from([1, 2, 3])))

fn run() i32:
    let Bag b = make()??.clone()
    println("n={b.items.len()} first={b.items[0]}")
    return Result.Ok(0)

fn main() i32:
    return Result.Ok(run().realise(99))
